Falls and instability
Geriatric & Frailty
Recurrent unprovoked falls, Postural instability/poor balance, Proximal lower limb weakness, Polypharmacy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-020
Reason for Visit: Acute distress following a recurrent, unprovoked fall resulting in an inability to get up from the floor independently.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a frail patient who had suffered a recurrent fall and was currently stranded on the floor, unable to bear weight due to profound weakness and postural instability. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out an acute fracture (e.g., neck of femur), an acute neurological event, or severe orthostatic hypotension, providing safe mobilization and preventing a long lie prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs just completely gave way again when I turned around, and I simply do not have the strength to push myself back up."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports three recurrent episodes of inadvertently coming to rest on the ground over the past month. They describe an unsteady, wide-based, and slow gait pattern recently. Today, while turning quickly in the kitchen, they experienced severe postural instability and extremely poor balance upon sudden head movements, resulting in a fall. They specifically deny dizziness, presyncope, or lightheadedness upon standing, ruling out classical orthostatic hypotension for this specific event. They report significant proximal muscle weakness, particularly in the lower extremities, which severely limits their ability to catch themselves or get up from the floor. They express a generalized intense fear of falling, which has led to self-imposed severe activity restriction over the past week.
Functional Status: Currently stranded on the floor; baseline mobilizes with a walking stick but struggles with transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of long lie complications (rhabdomyolysis, pressure sores, pneumonia); living alone with poor mobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Sitting on the floor, highly anxious, but alert, orientated, and in no acute respiratory or cardiac distress.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals no shortening, external rotation, or pain on passive internal rotation of either hip, suggesting the absence of a neck of femur fracture. No bony point tenderness along the spine or limbs. Neurological exam reveals symmetrical proximal lower limb weakness (power 4/5 in hip flexors and knee extensors) but intact sensation and normal reflexes. No focal deficits suggestive of an acute stroke. Once assisted to a standing position, the patient demonstrates a positive 'Timed Up and Go' test result significantly greater than 12 seconds, highlighting profound postural instability/poor balance.
Environmental Observations: The kitchen has poor lighting and several loose rugs that act as tripping hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient is subject to polypharmacy, including the recent addition of a nocturnal sedative (temazepam) and two specific antihypertensives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Recurrent Falls and Instability secondary to proximal weakness and polypharmacy - Status: High Risk
Differentials:

Acute Fractured Neck of Femur - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of hip pain on palpation or passive rotation, no leg shortening/external rotation, and ability to bear weight (with assistance) once standing.

Acute Stroke (CVA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical nature of the weakness without unilateral upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia, spasticity) or cranial nerve deficits.

Orthostatic Hypotension - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a significant blood pressure drop upon standing and lack of presyncopal symptoms (dizziness/vision greying) prior to the fall; the fall was mechanical/balance-related on turning.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a recurrent, unprovoked fall resulting in a distressing inability to self-recover from the floor, driven by proximal weakness and balance deficits. Telephone triage correctly identified the acute risk of a fracture or long lie. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient stranded on the floor, resulting in profound distress, hypothermia, pressure necrosis, and likely an emergency ambulance callout. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for a careful physical examination to confidently rule out a major fracture, safe mechanical assistance from the floor, and the immediate identification of heavily sedating polypharmacy as a modifiable risk factor, averting a hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Assisted the patient safely from the floor using proper manual handling techniques. Reassured regarding the absence of acute fracture. Immediately ceased the recently prescribed temazepam (sedative), which is highly likely contributing to the postural instability and proximal weakness.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured "sit-to-stand" exercise routine. Step 1: The patient practices standing up from a firm dining chair without using their arms to push off, repeating this five times before each meal. Step 2: Upon successful completion of this specific strengthening set, the patient rewards themselves with their scheduled meal and a hot drink. Cause-and-effect: Associating a critical, targeted proximal muscle strengthening exercise directly with the established routine of eating ensures high compliance, rapidly building the quadriceps strength necessary to prevent future falls and enable self-recovery.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a community physiotherapy falls prevention program and an occupational therapist for home modifications.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently recommended the removal of all loose rugs in the kitchen and the installation of a personal medical alarm pendant.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 3-5 days to comprehensively review all antihypertensive medications and formalize the allied health referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department via ambulance if the patient falls again and experiences severe pain in the hip or groin, is completely unable to bear weight, hits their head, or experiences a sudden loss of consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: No broken bones were found today, but your balance and strength are critically low; formal physiotherapy is required to keep you safe at home.
Clinical Course: The grogginess and balance issues should improve over the next 48 hours as the sleeping tablet washes out of your system. Muscle strength will only improve with dedicated daily exercises.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you fall again and cannot put any weight on your leg, if you hit your head, or if you feel severe pain in your hip. You must see your regular GP next week to review all your blood pressure tablets.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your fall was caused by a combination of weak thigh muscles and the lingering effects of your new sleeping tablet, which impairs your balance. We have stopped the sleeping tablet to clear your head. We now need to focus heavily on rebuilding the strength in your legs.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking the temazepam immediately. Remove all loose rugs from your floors tonight. Perform your "sit-to-stand" exercises five times before every meal to rebuild the muscle required to keep you upright.

